  "question": {
    "text": "The relation between time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi></math> and distance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> for a moving body is given as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> are constants. The retardation of the motion is: (When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math>&nbsp;stands for velocity)"
  },
